    <statement><p>Consider the function <m>f(x) = 4x-x^{2}</m>.</p>
<p>a. Use the limit definition to compute the derivative values: <m>f'(0), \ f'(1), \ f'(2),</m> and <m>f'(3)</m>.</p>
<p>Apply the limit definition: <m>\displaystyle f'(0) = \lim_{h\to 0} \Big[</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/><m>/h\Big]</m></p>
<p>Evaluate the limit: <m>f'(0)=</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>Apply the limit definition: <m>\displaystyle f'(1) = \lim_{h\to 0}\Big[</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0003"/><m>/h\Big]</m></p>
<p>Evaluate the limit: <m>f'(1)=</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p>Apply the limit definition: <m>\displaystyle f'(2) = \lim_{h\to 0}\Big[</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0005"/><m>/h\Big]</m></p>
<p>Evaluate the limit: <m>f'(2)=</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
<p>Apply the limit definition: <m>\displaystyle f'(3) = \lim_{h\to 0}\Big[</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0007"/><m>/h\Big]</m></p>
<p>Evaluate the limit: <m>f'(3)=</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0008"/></p>
<p>b. Observe that the work to find <m>f(a)</m> is the same, regardless of the value of 
<m>a</m>. Based
on your work in (a), what do you conjecture is the value of <m>f'(4)</m>? How about <m>f'(5)</m>? (Note: you should not use the limit definition of the derivative to find either value.)</p>
<p><m>f'(4) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0009"/></p>
<p><m>f'(5) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0010"/></p>
<p>c. Conjecture a formula for <m>f'(a)</m> that depends only on the value <m>a</m>. That is, in the
same way that we have a formula for <m>f(x)</m> (recall <m>f(x) = 4x-x^{2}</m>), see if you can
use your work above to guess a formula for <m>f'(a)</m> in terms of <m>a</m>.</p>
<p><m>f'(a) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0011"/></p>
</statement>
